Write a self-contained prompt that tells an AI coding agent to build a Python
CLI tool for Termux that manages git history: it reads and rewrites commits,
produces combined diffs, and works with a backend abstraction that falls back to
subprocess git calls.
Specify purpose, inputs and outputs, dependencies, behavior, error handling,
edge cases, and usage.
Do not include code.
Create a single-file Python 3 command-line script intended to run under Termux
on Android (shebang pointing to the Termux Python interpreter at
/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python).
The script is a git-history helper: it operates on a local git repository,
inspects recent commits, can produce a combined diff between two revisions, and
can perform history-rewriting operations on the last N commits.
It is organized around a backend abstraction.
Requirements for the script: **Language and dependencies** - Python 3 standard
library only: argparse, json, os, subprocess, sys, datetime.
Use `from __future__ import annotations` at the top. - No third-party packages.
Shell out to the `git` executable for all repository operations. **Module
structure** 1.
A module-level helper `run(cmd, cwd=None, capture=False, check=True)`: - When
`capture` is true, run with stdout and stderr piped, text mode enabled, and
return the completed process.
If `check` is true and the return code is nonzero, raise
`subprocess.CalledProcessError` including the return code, the command, the
captured stdout as output, and stderr. - When `capture` is false, run with
inherited stdio (output goes straight to the terminal).
If `check` is true and it fails, raise `CalledProcessError` with the return code
and command. - Return the completed process in both cases. 2.
A helper `is_git_repo(path=".")` that runs `git rev-parse --git-dir` in the
given directory with capture and returns True on success, False on any
exception. 3.
A `Backend` class constructed with a `repo_path` (default "."), storing the
absolute path and creating a `SubprocessBackend` instance (a separate class that
performs git operations by invoking the git CLI through `run`) as its
`fallback`.
The `Backend` class delegates these methods to the fallback: -
`ensure_clean_worktree(force=False)`: verify the working tree has no uncommitted
changes; if `force` is true, bypass the check. - `get_head()`: return the
current HEAD commit identifier. - `get_last_n_commits(n)`: return the last N
commit identifiers or records. - `write_combined_diff(base_rev, head_rev,
out_patch_path)`: write the combined diff between two revisions to a patch file
at the given path. - Additional rewrite-related operations on the last N commits
as needed by the CLI (for example, squashing, reordering, or rewriting commit
messages), each delegating to the fallback backend. - Design `SubprocessBackend`
so that each method runs git commands via `run` with the repo path as `cwd`,
capturing output when values are needed. 4.
A `main()` function with an `argparse`-based CLI that: - Accepts a repository
path (default current directory). - Provides subcommands or flags for: showing
HEAD, listing the last N commits, writing a combined diff between a base and
head revision to an output file, and performing history operations on the last N
commits. - Includes a `--force` option that is passed to `ensure_clean_worktree`
to allow operating on a dirty working tree. - Produces machine-readable output
as JSON when requested (use the `json` module), and human-readable text
otherwise. - Uses `datetime` to timestamp generated output or backup/patch file
names, so repeated runs do not overwrite each other by default. 5.
An `if __name__ == "__main__":` guard that calls `main()` and exits with its
return code via `sys.exit`. **Behavior and edge cases** - Before any mutating
operation, check that the path is a git repository (using `is_git_repo`) and,
unless `--force` is given, that the working tree is clean.
Exit with a clear error message and nonzero status if either check fails. - If
`N` exceeds the number of available commits, use only the commits that exist and
warn the user rather than crashing.
If N is less than 1, reject with an error. - If the repository has fewer than
two commits, a combined diff against a base revision that does not exist must
fail gracefully with a message. - Handle the root commit correctly when
generating diffs (compare against the empty tree, for example via the well-known
empty tree object). - Ensure `CalledProcessError` from git is caught in
`main()`, printing the git stderr to the user and returning a nonzero exit code,
rather than printing a raw traceback. - Catch `KeyboardInterrupt` and exit with
code 130. - Do not silently overwrite existing patch files; either append a
timestamp (from `datetime`) to the file name or refuse with an error unless an
explicit overwrite flag is given. - Validate that output directories exist
before writing patch files. - When running history rewrites, do not leave the
repository in a detached or half-rewritten state; if a git command fails midway,
report the failure and state where the repository was left. **Output and usage**
- Print progress and results to stdout; print errors to stderr. - Return exit
code 0 on success, 1 on handled errors, 2 for argument errors (argparse
default), and 130 on interrupt. - Include a `--help` description summarizing
each subcommand and option. - Keep comments sparse and idiomatic; follow
standard PEP 8 naming and formatting.
Deliver the complete script as a single file, with the Termux shebang line,
ready to run as `python <script> [options]` from inside a git repository. ---
